WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.948 --> 00:00:16.492
Laurie heard the beating of a drum coming from the study.

2
00:00:17.452 --> 00:00:22.034
Then she heard Grandpa call out, "Hear me, people, for I speak the truth!

3
00:00:23.174 --> 00:00:29.416
Tell this story to your children so the name Sundiata—the Lion King—lives forever!"

4
00:00:30.892 --> 00:00:34.154
"Grandpa!" Laurie pushed the study door open.

5
00:00:34.174 --> 00:00:37.075
"What are you doing?"

6
00:00:38.316 --> 00:00:40.878
Grandpa sat on the couch with a large drum.

7
00:00:42.018 --> 00:00:44.119
A stringed instrument was by his feet.

8
00:00:44.139 --> 00:00:50.243
"I'm practicing for my African storytelling performance.

9
00:00:51.323 --> 00:00:55.906
Hayside Elementary asked me to give a presentation to their third grade.

10
00:00:57.086 --> 00:00:59.908
I'll be telling them about the golden age of Africa."

11
00:01:02.144 --> 00:01:04.125
"But what are the instruments for?"

12
00:01:04.505 --> 00:01:05.306
Laurie asked.

13
00:01:06.446 --> 00:01:11.169
"African storytellers play instruments while sharing their legends and histories.

14
00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:15.891
It's usually this instrument called the kora and maybe a drum."

15
00:01:15.911 --> 00:01:17.892
"That's cool.

16
00:01:18.673 --> 00:01:19.834
What's the story about?"

17
00:01:20.114 --> 00:01:20.874
Laurie asked.

18
00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:30.839
"It's about three kingdoms of Africa—the Ghana, Mali, and Songhai kingdoms.

19
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.142
These developed on the edge of the Sahara Desert.

20
00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:42.087
During the Middle Ages, some of the richest kingdoms in the world developed there.

21
00:01:43.228 --> 00:01:51.074
Originally, people of the Sahara were desert nomads who wandered from oasis to oasis to find water and

22
00:01:51.154 --> 00:01:53.636
vegetation for themselves and their herds.

23
00:01:55.137 --> 00:02:02.425
But legend says they always returned to one oasis, where they left their heavy belongings with a woman

24
00:02:02.485 --> 00:02:03.806
named Buktu.

25
00:02:05.046 --> 00:02:10.309
Eventually this spot became a city called Timbuktu, named after the woman.

26
00:02:11.369 --> 00:02:19.593
By around 1100 AD, Timbuktu was a major city along the trading route that crossed the Sahara and

27
00:02:19.653 --> 00:02:22.254
carried goods from Africa to Arabia."

28
00:02:22.274 --> 00:02:25.975
"Isn't the Sahara Desert huge?"

29
00:02:26.376 --> 00:02:27.196
Laurie asked.

30
00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:30.850
"How did they cross it?"

31
00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:31.890
"It is huge.

32
00:02:32.811 --> 00:02:39.635
Imagine that you have to journey through it for days and nights carrying loads of gold, fabric, iron

33
00:02:39.755 --> 00:02:41.156
tools, and clay pots.

34
00:02:42.297 --> 00:02:43.077
What would you do?"

35
00:02:44.238 --> 00:02:46.239
Grandpa paused and looked at Laurie.

36
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.241
"Gather up your camels, of course!

37
00:02:50.442 --> 00:02:53.284
The Africans traveled by camel caravan.

38
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.783
These animals can walk through hot sand for days without food or water.

39
00:03:00.304 --> 00:03:06.267
So traders loaded up about a hundred camels with goods and then set off into the desert.

40
00:03:07.488 --> 00:03:10.489
Usually the trip to Arabia took about forty days.

41
00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:17.513
Can you guess what made the Africans so rich during their golden age?"

42
00:03:17.953 --> 00:03:19.554
Grandpa asked with a smile.

43
00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:22.656
"Gold!" Laurie responded.

44
00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.644
"Right! Gold and salt," Grandpa said.

45
00:03:28.664 --> 00:03:35.009
"Salt was so important back then that it had the same value as gold.

46
00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.213
Salt is used to keep food from spoiling.

47
00:03:40.314 --> 00:03:42.195
It also makes food taste good.

48
00:03:43.216 --> 00:03:46.439
But most importantly it is essential to human bodies.

49
00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:48.901
We can't live without salt!

50
00:03:49.942 --> 00:03:55.669
And when you sweat, you lose a lot of salt, so it was especially important to Africans who

51
00:03:55.729 --> 00:03:58.751
lived in a hot climate and sweated a lot every day.

52
00:04:00.351 --> 00:04:07.295
Salt and gold helped the first great African kingdom— the Ghana kingdom—come to power.

53
00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:13.377
Ghana's cities became home to the best artists and scholars on the continent.

54
00:04:14.438 --> 00:04:19.280
But eventually the Ghanaians were overrun by a group of Islamic Arabs.

55
00:04:20.468 --> 00:04:25.132
Wars broke out as different groups tried to gain control over the cities.

56
00:04:26.353 --> 00:04:33.819
Then a brave warrior named Sundiata, or 'hungering lion,' led his people to defeat the existing rulers.

57
00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:44.947
In 1235 he established the next great kingdom—the Mali kingdom— and created a stable government with a tax

58
00:04:44.987 --> 00:04:50.859
system. Under his rule, Mali grew richer and stronger every year.

59
00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:58.865
Then in 1307 another great leader, named Mansa Musa, came to the throne.

60
00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:03.508
He doubled the size of the kingdom and tripled its trade.

61
00:05:04.469 --> 00:05:08.672
Gold was flowing from Mali to Arabia, and then into Europe.

62
00:05:10.844 --> 00:05:18.971
Islamic Arabic traders had been trying to convert Africans to Islam for years, and Mali was the first

63
00:05:19.071 --> 00:05:23.015
kingdom to accept Islam as its religion.

64
00:05:23.235 --> 00:05:32.122
In 1324 Mansa Musa, a devoted Muslim, made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca, the birthplace of Islam.

65
00:05:33.243 --> 00:05:38.008
This pilgrimage gave Arabs their first image of how rich Mali was.

66
00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:44.504
Mansa Musa's caravan was the largest ever to travel across the Sahara.

67
00:05:45.725 --> 00:05:48.987
Five-hundred slaves walked before the great king.

68
00:05:50.568 --> 00:05:55.451
Mansa Musa, with his party of over fifty-thousand people, followed.

69
00:05:56.712 --> 00:06:02.456
One-hundred camels walked behind them, carrying over thirty-thousand pounds of gold.

70
00:06:03.657 --> 00:06:07.960
Mansa Musa was proud to present the riches of his kingdom to the Arabs.

71
00:06:09.028 --> 00:06:12.071
Everywhere he went he gave out beautiful gifts.

72
00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:17.716
He also went to markets and bought everything the merchants offered him.

73
00:06:18.857 --> 00:06:21.299
And he always paid in pure gold.

74
00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:25.583
Mansa Musa left quite an impression in Arabia.

75
00:06:27.145 --> 00:06:34.291
News of his incredible pilgrimage even traveled to Europe, where people were stunned by this rich king of

76
00:06:34.391 --> 00:06:39.989
Africa." "Did it make Europeans want to go to Africa too?"

77
00:06:40.349 --> 00:06:41.129
Laurie asked.

78
00:06:41.149 --> 00:06:42.750
"It did.

79
00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.171
But we'll get to that later.

80
00:06:46.351 --> 00:06:52.173
Still one more great African kingdom arose after Mali—the Songhai kingdom.

81
00:06:53.714 --> 00:07:00.456
Songhai was a territory that had been captured by Mali, and its people wanted their freedom back.

82
00:07:01.672 --> 00:07:12.059
In 1468 a Songhai leader named Sunni Ali Ber took over Timbuktu and gained control of the Saharan

83
00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:12.820
trading routes.

84
00:07:13.981 --> 00:07:19.624
The Mali kingdom fell apart, and the Songhai kingdom ruled West Africa.

85
00:07:20.585 --> 00:07:24.988
But no golden age lasts forever.

86
00:07:25.148 --> 00:07:29.931
In 1591 Muslims from Morocco attacked the Songhai kingdom.

87
00:07:31.212 --> 00:07:32.934
And you were absolutely right, Laurie.

88
00:07:33.994 --> 00:07:39.459
Learning about the rich African kingdoms made people want to take control of Africa.

89
00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:47.466
In the 1600s the Portuguese and Dutch began setting up their own cities and colonies there.

90
00:07:48.567 --> 00:07:50.028
But that's another story."

91
00:07:50.048 --> 00:07:55.773
"Wow, I never knew Africa had kingdoms like that," Laurie said.

92
00:07:55.793 --> 00:07:59.336
"Yeah," Grandpa said sadly.

93
00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:05.502
"Most people only learn about the slave trade that brought Africans to the Americas.

94
00:08:07.043 --> 00:08:10.464
But Africans have a much greater history to be proud of.

95
00:08:14.526 --> 00:08:16.446
And I will be proud to tell it."

96
00:08:17.567 --> 00:08:19.168
Grandpa tapped his drum.

97
00:08:19.188 --> 00:08:23.369
"Well, I have a great story of my own to tell."

98
00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:26.951
Laurie moved the drum between her legs and tapped it.

99
00:08:28.011 --> 00:08:29.472
"Listen to my story.

100
00:08:30.604 --> 00:08:33.448
Here are the adventures of a fourth-grader!"

101
00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:41.160
And she began to tell Grandpa about a brave young girl named Laurie and her wise grandfather who

102
00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:42.401
studied the past.
